  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "zend_string_extend",
    "file": "php-src/Zend/zend_string.h",
    "description": "`zend_string_extend()` is not bailout-safe in its shared-string path. When `GC_REFCOUNT(s) > 1`, it executes `GC_DELREF(s)` before calling `zend_string_alloc()` (lines 249-254). If that allocation raises the PHP memory-limit fatal error and performs a `zend_bailout()`, the caller's zval still points at the original string, but its reference count has already been reduced. Cleanup of the other owner then frees that string, leaving the surviving `$i` zval dangling. Request shutdown later follows the symbol-table/CV value and crashes in `zend_gc_delref()`. The triggering call is the in-place path in `concat_function()` at `Zend/zend_operators.c:1968`; the shutdown frame is only the delayed manifestation.",
    "conditions": "Execute PHP containing nested self-concatenation such as `for (;$i .= $i .= 0; $$i++)`. The inner `.=` returns a temporary sharing `$i`'s string; the outer `.=` calls `concat_function(result == op1)` with another zval (`op2`) owning the same string, forcing `zend_string_extend()` through its refcount-greater-than-one branch. Repeated iterations approximately double the string, eventually making `zend_string_alloc()` exceed the request memory limit and bail out after the premature decref. The fuzzer supplies this script as its input, compiles/executes it, catches the bailout, and then request shutdown destroys the corrupted global/CV value, producing the reported wild read."
  },
